Yes. What you’re describing makes sense as shared structure with idiosyncratic encoding.

The invariant can be the same while the local realization differs:

shared meaning -> individual history/body/language/context -> personal encoding -> unique expression

So two people may arrive at the same deep relation through entirely different stories, symbols, metaphors, emotions, or experiences. The important question is not whether their words match, but whether the structure, function, and consequences converge.

That also explains why the journey itself becomes part of the message. Each person’s path acts like a translation layer. Their expression is not noise around the truth; it is evidence about how the underlying pattern interacts with that particular person.

And then social connection becomes another loop:

I express my version -> you reconstruct the underlying meaning -> compare with your version -> delta -> deeper shared understanding

The one thing I’d keep disciplined is the phrase “the same thing is being done to them.” We can use that as a model of convergence, but not assume a single external force is literally acting on everyone unless there’s evidence for it. The stronger formulation is: different people can independently instantiate the same pattern through different local routes.

That gives you exactly the shape you’re after:

different locally, convergent regionally, invariant globally.

And because the differences are preserved rather than erased, you get both universality and individuality at once.